// THE KYBALION: A STUDY OF THE HERMETIC PHILOSOPHY OF ANCIENT EGYPT AND GREECE,
// BY "THREE INITIATES" (CHICAGO: THE YOGI PUBLICATION SOCIETY, 1908).
//
// The text comes from kybalion-text.ts, generated by scripts/texts/kybalion.py
// from Wikisource's proofread transcription of the Brigham Young University
// copy of 1908, voted word by word against the archive.org OCRs of that copy
// and of three other 1908 copies and against Project Gutenberg #14209 (a later
// printing). The majority reading is taken; every reading the vote took and
// every correction made by hand is listed in the script, and the doubtful
// places were read on the BYU page images. The whole book is imported: the
// Introduction and the fifteen chapters, one unit each, under a record for
// the book, with the sayings it quotes as "The Kybalion" left in place in
// their chapters (39 of them, listed in kybalion-text.ts).
//
// THE CLAIM AND THE BOOK. The Kybalion presents itself as the teaching of an
// unwritten collection of Hermetic maxims handed down from Hermes Trismegistus,
// "contemporary with Abraham". The archive records that as the book's claim
// and gives it no year. No such collection has been produced; the "Seven
// Hermetic Principles" are not in the Corpus Hermeticum or the Asclepius, and
// the maxims it quotes have no known ancient source. It is a New Thought book
// of 1908, usually attributed to William Walker Atkinson: its publisher, which
// registered the copyright, was his own firm. That is an attribution with
// evidence, not a signature, and is recorded as such. "As above, so below"
// echoes the second line of the Latin Emerald Tablet, which the book never
// names; the links say so, and none claims ancient transmission.
//
// Nothing is marked verified: the text is a careful copy, not a reading
// checked page by page against the book.

